  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>About a ten-minute walk from a small car park on the road to the Col de Turini, the Cascade du Piaon sits in a wooded valley above Sospel, with a pool whose jade-green reflections invite a swim in summer.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>From Sospel, take the D2566 heading north for about 5 km (past the campsite), and park at one of the two roadside spots. A fairly steep path, over about 200 m of elevation change, then leads down to the foot of the waterfall. In summer, the pool becomes a genuine natural swimming hole, busy in fine weather. The path is described by several independent sources as "very steep", hard to reach and slippery in wet weather; a TripAdvisor review advises against it for families. Parking is limited to two roadside spots, with no space to settle near the pool. The AllTrails guide lists the 0.3 km loop among routes for leashed dogs, with no official value.</p>
      <p>Worth checking before you go: the Cascades de France site says the waterfall is on private land and one visitor claims access is forbidden, while swimming sites present it as freely accessible; no official source (Sospel town hall or tourist office) settles it. No official rule about dogs was found either: ask the Sospel tourist office before setting off.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the access path, short but steep, can get slippery for a dog after rain — bring a short leash for the descent.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The village of Sospel, with its old bridge and market, pairs well with an outing to the Cascade du Piaon; the road to the Col de Turini then continues toward the Vésubie valley.</p>
